# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Tlock tag[2] fid[4] type[1] flags[4] start[8] length[8]
        proc_id[4] client_id[s]
size[4] Rlock tag[2] status[1]

size[4] Tgetlock tag[2] fid[4] type[1] start[8] length[8]
        proc_id[4] client_id[s]
size[4] Rgetlock tag[2] type[1] start[8] length[8] proc_id[4]
        client_id[s]
```

# DESCRIPTION

These are 9P2000.L's messages, unchanged on the wire, and belong to the `posix` extension: a server answers them only once `Tversion` has negotiated `+posix` (see version(5)), and before that with `operation not supported`.
`Tlock` is type 52, `Rlock` 53, `Tgetlock` 54 and `Rgetlock` 55.
As every 9Px message does, they fail with an `Rerror` and its string, not 9P2000.L's `Rlerror` and an errno (see error(5)).

A lock is POSIX's, as `fcntl` takes it: a read lock or a write lock over a range of a file's bytes, `[`<start>`, `<start>` + `<length>`)`, or from <start> to the end of the file and past it when <length> is 0.
Its <type> is 0 for a read lock, 1 for a write lock, and 2 for none, unlock.
Locks are advisory: they stop other locks, never reads or writes.
Two locks on a file conflict when their owners differ, their ranges overlap, and either is a write lock.

The server keeps them, in one table of 256 shared by all its connections.
A lock's owner is the connection and <proc_id> together: two processes on one connection with different <proc_id>s are different owners, and so are two connections with the same one.
<client_id> is ignored.
A connection's locks on a file go when it lets go of any fid it has open on that file, by `Tclunk` or `Tremove`, a new `Tversion`, or hanging up: every one of its locks on the file, whatever their <proc_id>, as a POSIX process's go when it closes any descriptor of the file.
Every VectraOS server with `posix` keeps locks, read-only ones too.

<fid> must be in use on the connection, or the answer is `unknown fid`, and open, or the answer is `fid already in use`.
<type> past 2 is refused with `bad message`, and a range whose end does not fit in 64 bits with `offset out of range`.

## Lock

The `lock` request takes a lock of <type> over the range for the owner, replacing whatever that owner held of the range; unlock lets go of it.
<fid> may be open in any mode: fcntl's rule, a read lock on a file open for reading and a write lock on one open for writing, is the client's to keep, as Linux's kernel keeps it, and `flock` has none (M6 step 6d9a).

`Rlock`'s <status> is one of:

: 0, success
  The owner now holds the range as asked: its old locks there, of either type, are gone, cut short or split around it, and the new one is in their place.
  Locks beside each other are not joined.
: 1, blocked
  Another owner's lock is in the way, and nothing has changed.
: 2, error
  The table has no room for the new lock, or for the second piece of one of the owner's locks that it would split, and nothing has changed.

The server never waits: <flags>, 9P2000.L's `BLOCK` (1) and `RECLAIM` (2), is ignored, and a lock in the way is always answered blocked at once.
9P2000.L's fourth status, grace, is never sent.

## Getlock

The `getlock` request asks whether the owner could take a lock of <type> over the range.
`Rgetlock` names the first lock in the server's table that is in the way: its <type>, its <start>, its <length> (0 if it runs to the end), and its owner's <proc_id>, with an empty <client_id>.
If none is, or <type> is unlock, `Rgetlock` has <type> 2 and the request's <start>, <length>, <proc_id> and <client_id>.
<fid> may be open in any mode.

## The client

VectraOS's client sends <flags> 0 and an empty <client_id>, and only on a connection that negotiated `posix`.
The POSIX library's `fcntl` sends `Tgetlock` for `F_GETLK` and `Tlock` for `F_SETLK` and `F_SETLKW`, with the process's id as <proc_id>; POSIX's `F_RDLCK`, `F_WRLCK` and `F_UNLCK` are 0, 1 and 2 there too.
For `F_SETLK` blocked is `EAGAIN`, and for `F_SETLKW` the library sends the request again every 10 ms until it succeeds or a signal comes; error is `ENOLCK`.
It lets go of its fid when the last of the process's descriptors for the open file closes, and sends an unlock of the whole file when another of them closes.

